--TEST--
A `readonly` property declares no default, while a property that is not `readonly` keeps one (`rule:php-migration/a-readonly-property-declares-no-default`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:php-migration/a-readonly-property-declares-no-default`: `readonly` is
// one assignment during construction, so a property whose single assignment is
// its own declaration-site default is a per-instance constant — and `const`
// already spells one. The plain property below keeps its default, so the
// refusal reads the modifier and not the `=`.
class Config {
    public int $retries = 3;
    public readonly int $timeout = 30;
}

var $c = new Config();
echo $c->retries, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0249]: a `readonly` property declares no default
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
